Hello,
i've got a problem...

I'm using an AlphaStation 200  4/233 with RedHat 7.1
I'm booting throught the SRM console with auto_action set to BOOT.

When I issue a shutdown (it doesn't matter if I use -h or -r) the systems 
goes down and then switches back to the SRM screen. Then it dumps a lot of 
numbers (the processor registers and some other stuff i wasn't able to 
recognize) and then the system reboots if I had power-cycled the machine.

My problem is that I want my machine to HALT after I issue a shutdown, i.e. 
that I get my SRM prompt when the shutdown is complete and I want still be 
able to boot the machine automaticly when it switch it on (auto_action boot)

This MUST be possible somehow. I was using SuSE 6.4 before and when I issued 
a shutdown from SuSE it went down and then I got the SRM prompt. The 
differnce was that SuSE didn't dump the registers and the other stuff.

The output from SRM when shutdown is finished is:

halted CPU 0

halt code=5
HALT instruction executed
PC = ....

CPU 0 booting

execption context saved starting at 00110A50

GPRs:
.......

and so on

my old SuSE showed me the SRM prompt after the PC = or the CPU 0 booting line 
(I'm not sure).


Does anyone of you know how i can  manage to get back to the SRM console 
without changing auto_action

Thanks in advance 

Gregor

-- 
Gregor Maier
gregor.maier@gmx.at




